WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.509
Numbers quickly get very large when you start adding zeros to the end of them.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:08.580
The problem is that our brains aren't naturally wired to comprehend that kind of scale.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.921
And at a certain point they become these weird, intangible concepts

00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:13.832
rather than something you can actually count.

00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:15.404
Take for instance, a billion.

00:00:15.404 --> 00:00:17.668
It's a very, very large number, but,

00:00:17.668 --> 00:00:19.018
..so is a million.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:22.497
I mean, they sound kinda similar so don't they kinda feel similar?

00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:23.721
But imagine this:

00:00:23.721 --> 00:00:27.256
One million seconds adds up to about a week and a half.

00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:30.660
Now I want you to take a guess at how long a billion seconds might be.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.709
-How's it going, Wren?

00:00:31.709 --> 00:00:33.292
-How long is a billion seconds?

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:34.343
-Three weeks.

00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:35.129
- Six months?

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:36.108
- 63 days.

00:00:36.108 --> 00:00:36.446
- Three...

00:00:36.446 --> 00:00:37.327
- You can't think of that!

00:00:37.327 --> 00:00:38.069
- Five years.

00:00:38.069 --> 00:00:38.918
- Two fortnights.

00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:39.749
- Three fortnights?

00:00:39.749 --> 00:00:40.550
- Four fortnights.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:41.457
- God dammit!

00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:43.367
One billion seconds

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.040
is 31.7 years.

00:00:53.804 --> 00:00:56.001
Which does make sense when you think about it,

00:00:56.001 --> 00:00:59.475
because, obviously a billion is a thousand times larger than a million.

00:01:00.069 --> 00:01:01.884
So let's begin by taking about some money.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:03.391
Man, hey.

00:01:03.391 --> 00:01:04.687
Look what I've got here.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:06.742
Have you ever seen...

00:01:06.742 --> 00:01:08.718
...a million dollars in cash?

00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:09.590
- Not personally.

00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:10.834
Woah...

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:12.257
Oh. D- don't worry about that.

00:01:12.257 --> 00:01:13.932
How can you possibly fit so much money

00:01:13.932 --> 00:01:15.284
in a Hogwarts bag?

00:01:15.284 --> 00:01:16.066
*laughs*

00:01:20.027 --> 00:01:21.876
This is 150,000 dollars.

00:01:21.876 --> 00:01:23.299
- I only got 140,000...

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:26.203
The reason why a million dollars doesn't seem all that imposing

00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:28.626
is because you're only looking at about 10,000 individual bills.

00:01:28.626 --> 00:01:31.217
So if this is a million dollars in cash,

00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:33.578
Can you imaging what a billion dollars in cash looks like?

00:01:33.578 --> 00:01:35.106
How big do you think that pile would be?

00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:37.231
- That's a lot of bills.
- That's a lot.

00:01:37.805 --> 00:01:39.623
That'd probably weigh this whole studio.

00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:41.786
So you might be surprised to learn that a billion dollars

00:01:41.786 --> 00:01:43.825
isn't gonna take up all that much space.

00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:45.585
Remember that show Breaking Bad?

00:01:45.585 --> 00:01:47.488
"You're God damn right."

00:01:47.488 --> 00:01:49.892
They had like, 80 million dollars worth of cash

00:01:49.892 --> 00:01:52.305
hidden in a storage locker at one point during the show

00:01:52.305 --> 00:01:53.847
and Bill Burr needed a nap.

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:55.181
"We are here to do a job,"

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:56.925
