00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.903
But that's only because these hundred dollar bills have a very high density of value.

00:02:21.903 --> 00:02:25.857
And we have yet to see the true magnitude of the number one billion.

00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:28.651
Pretend with me for a moment and let's imagine that you are Bill Gates.

00:02:28.651 --> 00:02:30.813
And you wanted to withdraw all of your net worth,

00:02:30.813 --> 00:02:34.454
and put it into a giant stack of 100 dollar bills in your backyard.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:36.044
On your tennis court, for instance.

00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:37.588
Of course you have a tennis court in your backyard.

00:02:37.588 --> 00:02:39.207
You're Bill Gates. You probably have thirty.

00:02:39.207 --> 00:02:43.776
Well, it would fill the entire inbound area of the court 19 feet high.

00:02:43.776 --> 00:02:46.410
This is one hundred billion dollars.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.279
Now, a single individual bank note weighs one gram.

00:02:49.279 --> 00:02:52.543
So, a billion grams is how much this entire stack weighs.

00:02:52.543 --> 00:02:56.596
That is the weight of 170 fully grown african elephants.

00:02:56.596 --> 00:02:57.888
Or ten blue whales.

00:02:57.888 --> 00:03:00.274
That seems like it can't possibly be true, but

00:03:00.274 --> 00:03:01.754
there it is. That's the math.

00:03:01.754 --> 00:03:03.282
